<Base title="Hot Tub Guides – Filters, Water Care, Covers & Supplies | MySpaFilter" description="Cited guides for hot tub filters and water care: sizes, fittings, Hot Spring, Caldera, Bullfrog, Sundance, Intex, cleaning, replacement and supplies.">
<p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › Guides</p>
<h1>Hot tub guides</h1>
<p>These guides answer the questions that come up after you pull a cartridge out of a hot tub: which number to trust, why two filters with the same diameter are not the same part, how often to clean, and what a flow error is reporting. Every size, part number and safety statement is tied to a published source. The sources are manufacturer manuals and catalog pages (Unicel, Pleatco, Filbur, Hot Spring, Jacuzzi, Sundance, Intex, Bestway), dealer listings we name on the cartridge page, and CDC or CPSC pages where the topic is health or chemical safety. If a catalog does not give a number, the guide says the column is blank.</p>


<h2>When one number is not enough</h2>
<p>Read the end cap first. An OEM number such as Hot Spring 71825, Caldera 1019401, Sundance 6540-488 or Intex 29001E beats a photo of “a spa filter.” <a href="/">Search it</a>: OEM, Unicel, Pleatco and Filbur numbers all work, and so does a diameter × length when the number is gone.</p>
<p>A search result can still be ambiguous in a few repeating ways:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Same outside size, different ends.</strong> Caldera’s 7" × 10-3/4" cartridges include a 2" male slip bottom and a 2-11/16" open bottom. The <a href="/guides/open-end-vs-threaded-hot-tub-filters/">open-end versus threaded</a> guide and the <a href="/guides/caldera-filter-bottom-fittings/">Caldera bottoms</a> guide are the pair to read.</li>
<li><strong>Same OEM number, different detail.</strong> Sundance C-8326 and C-8327 share 6540-488 and a 125 sq ft body; Unicel splits them on a core plug. Jacuzzi OEM 373045 is printed on both a 25 sq ft and a 50 sq ft cartridge. See <a href="/guides/sundance-and-jacuzzi-look-alike-filters/">look-alike Sundance and Jacuzzi filters</a> and <a href="/guides/why-same-size-spa-filters-have-different-square-feet/">why square-foot ratings differ</a>.</li>
<li><strong>The brand changed the cartridge by year.</strong> Hot Spring Highlife, earlier Limelight and 2018-and-later Limelight are three different bodies. A 2014 Caldera Cantabria depends on the production quarter. Start with <a href="/guides/hot-spring-filter-sizes-by-collection/">Hot Spring sizes by collection</a> or the model-year lookups for <a href="/fitment/hot-spring-filter-by-model-year/">Hot Spring</a>, <a href="/fitment/caldera-filter-by-model-year/">Caldera</a>, <a href="/fitment/jacuzzi-filter-by-model-year/">Jacuzzi</a>, <a href="/fitment/sundance-filter-by-model-year/">Sundance</a>, <a href="/fitment/bullfrog-filter-by-model-year/">Bullfrog</a> and <a href="/fitment/master-spas-filter-by-model-year/">Master Spas</a>.</li>
<li><strong>More than one cartridge in the well.</strong> Some Hot Spring listings call for three of the 6" cartridge. Unicel says later Master Spas Eco-Pur systems require two outer cartridges plus an inner element. Sundance pairs a pleated filter with a disposable insert. <a href="/guides/spas-that-use-more-than-one-filter/">Spas that use more than one filter</a> separates that from a spare set on the shelf.</li>
<li><strong>An inflatable spa.</strong> Intex Type S1 and Type S2 are different cartridges, and the letters on the set number decide which list you are on. Bestway Type VI is a third size, with a Monte Carlo exception. See <a href="/guides/intex-and-bestway-inflatable-spa-filters/">Intex and Bestway filters</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Same body, two square-foot ratings, or a maker that publishes only the area.</strong> Beachcomber’s 25 and 50 sq ft microfilters are one size from 1995 on. Strong’s FAQ names 50 or 75 sq ft by series and year and does not name a part. Lookups: <a href="/fitment/beachcomber-filter-by-model-year/">Beachcomber by year</a> and <a href="/fitment/strong-spas-filter-by-model-year/">Strong Spas by series and year</a>.</li>
<li><strong>The catalogs assign one model name to two cartridges.</strong> Freeflow TLX and RLX are the open cartridge in Unicel and the closed cartridge in Filbur and Pleatco. Freedom’s Franklin and Statesman are named on both a 4-5/8" cartridge and a 6" cartridge. Lookups: <a href="/fitment/freeflow-filter-by-model-year/">Freeflow by model</a> and <a href="/fitment/freedom-spas-filter-by-model-year/">Freedom Spas by model</a>.</li>
<li><strong>A factory chart and the catalogs disagree on the area.</strong> PDC’s 2025 guide rates the River cartridge at 25 sq ft and names Unicel C-4405. Unicel’s cross-reference and Filbur print 50 sq ft for that body as a two-cartridge set. The XD2 name is on the open cartridge and, from 2024, on a threaded cold-side cartridge. Lookup: <a href="/fitment/pdc-spas-filter-by-model-year/">PDC Spas by series and year</a>.</li>
<li><strong>The body matches and the thread does not.</strong> Coleman’s 7" cartridges come in 1-1/2" MPT and 2" MPT at the same length. Unicel pairs a 3301 number (through 2001) with a 100xxx number (since 2002) and does not name the spa model. Lookup: <a href="/fitment/coleman-spas-filter-by-model-year/">Coleman by part number</a>. Maax’s Elegant, Intrigue and Joli share one 25 sq ft cartridge, and Elite is the row Unicel prints at both 35 and 50 sq ft. Lookup: <a href="/fitment/maax-spas-filter-by-model-year/">Maax by model</a>. Saratoga’s 6-3/4" cartridge is 1-1/2" MPT (20241-238) or 1-1/4" SAE (4CH-920), and the catalogs print it at 15, 20, or 25 sq ft. Lookup: <a href="/fitment/saratoga-spas-filter-by-model-year/">Saratoga by part number</a>.</li>
<li><strong>The housing, not the spa model, picks the cartridge.</strong> Waterway’s front-access well is one 817-0050, two of them side by side, or a stack of 817-0011 on 817-0012. Lookup: <a href="/fitment/waterway-filter-by-model-year/">Waterway by housing</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Brand tables: <a href="/hot-spring/">Hot Spring</a>, <a href="/caldera/">Caldera</a>, <a href="/bullfrog/">Bullfrog</a>, <a href="/sundance/">Sundance</a>, <a href="/jacuzzi/">Jacuzzi</a>, <a href="/master-spas/">Master Spas</a>, <a href="/intex/">Intex</a>, <a href="/bestway/">Bestway</a>, <a href="/beachcomber/">Beachcomber</a>, <a href="/strong-spas/">Strong Spas</a>, <a href="/freeflow-spas/">Freeflow Spas</a>, <a href="/freedom-spas/">Freedom Spas</a>, <a href="/pdc-spas/">PDC Spas</a>, <a href="/coleman-spas/">Coleman Spas</a>, <a href="/maax-spas/">Maax Spas</a>, <a href="/waterway-plastics/">Waterway</a>, <a href="/saratoga-spas/">Saratoga Spas</a>.</p>

<h2>What we will not put in a guide</h2>
<p>We do not invent a part number to fill a blank column, and we do not average two catalogs when they print different lengths or different square-foot ratings. Softub OEM 90-802 is the clear example: Unicel and Filbur rate it at 25 sq ft and Pleatco rates it at 20, while the size and the thread agree. The guide says both. We also do not publish prices or star ratings. Product links are either a listing whose title we opened and checked, or an Amazon search for the part number. The tag on those links is this site’s Associates tag. Commissions do not change a cross-reference row. The <a href="/about/#methodology">methodology and corrections policy</a> explain how to report a number that does not match your cartridge.</p>
<p>Cleaning and replacement copy is limited to what the cited manual says. Hot Spring, Jacuzzi and Sundance do not all use one interval, and the guides keep the differences. Flow-error codes depend on who made the control system (Gecko, Balboa, Sundance, Hot Spring), so the <a href="/guides/hot-tub-flow-error-codes-and-your-filter/">flow-error guide</a> names the code and the document, and <a href="/guides/clogged-or-missing-hot-tub-filter/">clogged or missing filters</a> explains why the manuals say not to run the spa with the cartridge out.</p>
<p>Water-care guides are grouped on <a href="/supplies/">hot tub supplies</a>. They cite CDC and Hot Spring ranges and then list products. They are not a substitute for the owner’s manual, which is also what the <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a> says.</p>

{sections.map((s) => (
  <section><h2>{s.h}</h2><p>{s.blurb}</p>
  <ul class="grid">{s.slugs.map((slug) => { const g = by(slug); return <li class="card"><a href={`/guides/${g.slug}/`} style="font-weight:750;font-size:1.08rem">{g.title}</a><p class="crumbs">{g.desc}</p></li>; })}</ul></section>
))}
